Artificial Intelligence of Things (AIoT) applications frequently operate in environments that impose strict real-time requirements while subjecting the low-end embedded hardware to significant thermal stress. Cooling is often inadequate or absent for cost reasons or due to the interference of cooling subsystems (e.g., fans) with certain sensing modalities, such as acoustic and vibration sensing. Using a real-world real-time vehicle classification system as an illustrative example, we observe that low CPU frequencies fail to meet tight inference deadlines, while high frequencies present a risk of overheating and subsequent shutdown or unpredictable emergency throttling. To ensure predictable real-time operation, we model AIoT workloads as gang tasks with configurable parallelism and show that parallel execution strongly influences both timing and thermal behavior. We then propose a thermal-aware scheduling framework based on strict partitioning, extending the approach to consider both timing and thermal constraints. Towards that end, we formulate a multidimensional parallel load balancing problem, introduce a thermal-aware feasibility bound, and derive a polynomial-time near-optimal algorithm with a guaranteed approximation ratio, identifying sufficient feasibility conditions for meeting both timing and thermal constraints. Experiments with real-world benchmarks and a case study based on the vehicle classification application demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach in balancing stringent timing requirements against thermal limitations.